   "user": "Hazor",
   "message": "**How can two people know that they mean the same thing when they use the same word?**\n\nMatching the words is insufficient; their references and the comparisons they permit must agree. The same problem appears when two instruments report measurements, when coordinates change, or when a computer transforms a representation.\n\n**What must survive a change of representation for us to still be describing the same relationship?**\n\nThis connects language to mathematics without reducing language to equations: a word, a measurement and a coordinate can each carry a relationship while leaving its construction unstated. The paper examines those hidden commitments and makes their preservation checkable.\n\nThe distinction between reality, measurement and notation is therefore essential:\n- Reality: the interacting system being investigated.\n- Measurement: an interaction that establishes a comparison against a reference.\n- Notation: a representation of the resulting comparison, with rules for using it.\nThese are different roles, even though the instrument, observer and written symbols are themselves physical things. We can ground representation physically without giving every imagined referent a location.",
   "timestamp": "2026-09-16T16:14:02.275000+00:00",
   "channel": "✍️walter-log",
   "channel_id": "discord:1392675564074176744",
